    sources: [{"label_ja": "CNBC「How 'cash stuffing' is helping TikTok creators beat inflation, pay debt」（2022年4月20日）", "label_en": "CNBC, “How 'cash stuffing' is helping TikTok creators beat inflation, pay debt” (Apr 20, 2022)", "url": "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/04/20/how-cash-stuffing-is-helping-tiktok-creators-beat-inflation-pay-debt.html", "publisher": "CNBC"}, {"label_ja": "Prelec & Loewenstein「The Red and the Black: Mental Accounting of Savings and Debt」Marketing Science 17(1), 1998", "label_en": "Prelec & Loewenstein, “The Red and the Black: Mental Accounting of Savings and Debt,” Marketing Science 17(1), 1998", "url": "https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/mksc.17.1.4", "publisher": "INFORMS"}],
    faq: [{"q": {"ja": "キャッシュスタッフィングと袋分け家計簿は違うものですか？", "en": "Is cash stuffing different from Japanese envelope budgeting?"}, "a": {"ja": "考え方はほぼ同じです。どちらも使う現金を先に項目ごとに分け、その範囲で生活します。違いは呼び名と見せ方で、キャッシュスタッフィングはバインダーやラベルを使い、その過程をSNSで公開する文化が伴っています。日本の袋分け家計簿は封筒や通帳ケースを使い、家庭内で完結してきました。仕組みとしての中身は共通です。", "en": "They are essentially the same method. Both divide spending money into categories in advance and confine you to what each holds. The differences are naming and presentation: cash stuffing uses binders and labels and comes with a culture of filming the process for social media, while fukuro-wake kakeibo has traditionally used plain envelopes or passbook cases and stayed inside the home. Mechanically, they are one idea."}}, {"q": {"ja": "袋分け家計簿の項目はどう決めればいいですか？", "en": "How should I choose my envelope categories?"}, "a": {"ja": "最初は食費・外食費・交際費の3つで十分です。項目を細かくしすぎると管理が続かず、月半ばで放棄しがちになります。慣れてきたら日用品・趣味・美容などを追加し、多くても6〜7項目までに抑えるのが現実的です。基準は「自分の意志で金額が変わる費目か」で、家賃や保険料のような固定費は封筒に入れる意味が薄くなります。", "en": "Three is enough to start: groceries, eating out, and personal or social spending. Splitting into many categories makes the system fragile and people usually abandon it mid-month. Add household goods, hobbies or beauty once the habit holds, and cap it around six or seven. The test is whether your own decisions change the amount — fixed costs like rent or insurance premiums gain nothing from being placed in an envelope."}}, {"q": {"ja": "クレジットカード払いがある場合はどう管理しますか？", "en": "How do I handle credit card spending?"}, "a": {"ja": "カードで支払った日に、同じ金額をその費目の封筒から抜き、引き落とし用の封筒か別口座に移します。こうすると封筒の残りが常に「今月まだ使える額」を示し、口座残高とのずれも起きません。交通系ICやコード決済へのチャージも、チャージ時点で費目から抜いたものとして扱えば二重計上を防げます。", "en": "On the day you pay by card, remove the same amount from that category's envelope and move it to a settlement envelope or a separate account. The cash left in the envelope then always reflects what you can still spend this month, and the paper never drifts from your bank balance. Treat top-ups to a transit IC card or a QR payment app the same way — the money leaves the category at the moment of the top-up, which avoids double counting."}}, {"q": {"ja": "キャッシュレス中心の生活でも袋分けはできますか？", "en": "Can this work if I barely use cash?"}, "a": {"ja": "できます。袋分けの本質は現金そのものではなく、費目ごとの上限を先に決めて見える形にすることだからです。固定費はカードや口座振替のまま、変動費だけを封筒にするハイブリッドが現実的です。現金を持ち歩きたくない場合は、後述の家計簿アプリで費目別の予算枠を設定する方法でも同じ考え方を再現できます。向き不向きには個人差があります。", "en": "Yes. The substance of the method is not the banknotes but setting and displaying a ceiling per category in advance. A hybrid is the practical form: keep fixed costs on cards and direct debit, and put only variable spending into envelopes. If you would rather not carry cash at all, setting per-category budget limits in a budgeting app reproduces the same logic. How well it suits you depends on your circumstances."}}, {"q": {"ja": "家計簿アプリで代用できますか？", "en": "Can a budgeting app replace the envelopes?"}, "a": {"ja": "マネーフォワードMEやZaimのような家計簿アプリでも、費目ごとに予算枠を設定すれば袋分けの考え方を再現できます。ただし現金を数えて手渡す感覚は失われるため、支払いの実感を取り戻したい人には紙の封筒のほうが向く場合があります。どちらが続くかは個人差が大きいので、1か月ずつ試して残った方を選ぶのが確実です。", "en": "Budgeting apps such as MoneyForward ME or Zaim let you set per-category budget limits, which reproduces the envelope logic in software and removes most of the manual recording. What you lose is the physical act of counting and handing over cash, so if your goal is to feel your spending again, paper may serve better. Which one you actually sustain varies a lot by person — try each for a month and keep the survivor."}}, {"q": {"ja": "封筒が月末を待たずに空になったらどうすればいいですか？", "en": "What if an envelope empties before the month ends?"}, "a": {"ja": "その月は補充せず、そのカテゴリの支出を止めるのが基本ルールです。他の封筒から借りると仕組みが崩れます。ただし毎月同じ封筒が早く空になるなら、配分額が実態より低いというサインなので、翌月はその費目を増やし別の費目から減らして調整してください。最初の1〜2か月は削るのではなく実額を確定させる期間と考えると続きやすくなります。", "en": "The default rule is to stop spending in that category rather than refilling it, since borrowing from another envelope dissolves the mechanism. But if the same envelope empties early every month, that is a signal your allocation is below reality: raise it next month and lower another category to compensate. Treating the first month or two as measurement rather than reduction makes the habit far more likely to stick."}}],
